    "updated_at": "2025-06-11T01:05:11Z",
    "body": "Is this solved yet? How will import maps integrate with `<link>` exactly?\n\n---\n\nOne approach to keeping code DRY is outputting some JS in the HTML (by hand, or from backend):\n\n```html\n<script>\n// make a block scope to avoid leaking variables globally\n{\n  // make a variable with the importmap\n  const importmap = {\n    imports: {'foo/': 'path/to/foo/'},\n  }\n\n  // write the importmap synchronously\n  document.write(`<script type=\"importmap\">\n    ${JSON.stringify(importmap)}\n  <\\/script>`)\n\n  // re-use the importmmap programmatically for link preloads:\n  document.write(`<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"${importmap.imports['foo/']}/path/to/loadind.js\" \\/>`)\n}\n</script>\n\n<!-- This could be useful especially for loading experiences that need to load as soon as possible: -->\n<script type=\"module\" async>\n  import 'foo/path/to/loading-anim.js'\n</script>\n\n<loading-anim></loading-anim>\n\n<!-- load the rest of the app normally -->\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import 'foo/the-app.js'\n</script>\n\n<the-app></the-app>\n```\n\nwhere `<loading-anim>` may define a self-contained custom element without any dependencies.\n\nLive example: https://lume.io\n\n(Here's the self-contained  [loading icon source code](https://github.com/lume/lume/blob/develop/src/examples/LoadingIcon.ts), it is a custom element with type definitions because it is also useful in the app code during runtime, not only for the initial payload.)\n\nNote, put the importmap code in a separate file to avoid having to escape the backslashes.",
